    {
      "category": "Economic",
      "movement": "significant",
      "text": "More than 30,000-40,000 Samsung Electronics workers rallied at the Pyeongtaek chip facility. The National Samsung Electronics Union (NSEU) declared an 18-day strike beginning May 21 through June 7, which it estimates would cost Samsung 18 trillion won in losses. Production has already declined. Worker demands center on performance bonuses comparable to SK Hynix (which is expected to deliver up to 1.2 billion won per worker based on record profits). Samsung has offered more than 10% of operating profit allocated to a performance bonus pool but the union rejected this as a one-time gesture. More than 200 Samsung engineers moved to SK Hynix between January and April alone, concentrated in HBM packaging divisions. The Labor Minister characterized Samsung as a 'people's company' and acknowledged worker demands had merit. Samsung filed for an injunction against the union on April 16. An analyst (WCCFTech) estimated a 30 trillion won impact from a prolonged stoppage — not independently corroborated.",
